There's a big difference here, and I thought of this (unfortunately)
after making some of the prior posts.

If you're hit by lightning, you run a high risk of death.  And dead in
the real world means dead, not DYWYPI.

If you hit the 1-in-lots chance of an exploding trap on the castle wand
chest, your game converts temporarily to "wishless".  You don't even
die or lose any of your stuff!  You just are now playing semi-wishless 
conduct... and you still have the chance to find a wand on the ground
later.

> If somebody's had a string of strange luck and now prefers to manage 
> things differently, in a way that costs nothing or near it, let them.

And, as I said to ctaboir:  If you want to do it in your own game, I
can't stop you, but if you're going to present it as advice, then I'm
going to step in and correct you.

I think it's better for people to learn how to play, rather than to
learn to be scared to death of the game.  And I think advice like my
interlocutors have given teaches people the second.  (This is not the
first time it's come up in RGRN, of course, and, sadly, I'm sure it 
won't be the last.)

Being cautious about things that "if you're unlucky could kill you" is
one thing; none of us gets more than one go-round, and as such it makes
no sense to take a chance wasting it.  But this is a game, and the
effects in the game aren't even "death", they're just "one less useful 
resource."

(I would step in and correct you, as well, if you attempted to present
your own family history of being hit by lightning as anything other than
a statistical anomaly -- ie. "You should expect to be hit by lightning
at some point, all my family has been."  You didn't, so this is moot;
but it should adequately explain my position.)

